Check if the provided email address is valid. For example, abc.123@def.ghi.com.uk is a valid email address. Cover as many cases as possible to avoid false negatives.

@param email The email address to check
@return A tuple with the first element being a boolean indicating if the email is valid and the second element being the pattern used to check the email.
*/
std::tuple<bool, std::string> check_email(const std::string& email) {
    // This regex aims to cover a broad range of valid email addresses while rejecting common invalid formats.
    // It's a practical compromise between strict RFC compliance (which is extremely complex for a single regex)
    // and avoiding false negatives for commonly used valid email patterns.

    // Breakdown of the regex:
    // ^                                  - Start of the string.

    // Local Part (before @):
    // [a-zA-Z0-9.!#$%&'*+/=?^_`{|}~-]+
    // - [a-zA-Z0-9]      : Allows alphanumeric characters.
    // - .!#$%&'*+/=?^_`{|}~- : Allows a wide range of special characters commonly found in email local parts.
    // - +                : Ensures at least one character is present in the local part.
    // This part is intentionally permissive to avoid false negatives for valid but less common local part characters.
    // While RFCs have stricter rules (e.g., disallowing consecutive dots or leading/trailing dots),
    // this practical regex accepts many such cases to maximize compatibility with real-world email addresses.

    // @                                  - The literal at-sign separator.

    // Domain Part (after @):
    // (?:[a-zA-Z0-9](?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]*[a-zA-Z0-9])?\\.)+
    // - (?:...)          : A non-capturing group for domain labels.
    // - [a-zA-Z0-9]      : A domain label must start with an alphanumeric character.
    // - (?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]*[a-zA-Z0-9])? : Optional middle part of the label.
    //   - [a-zA-Z0-9-]*  : Zero or more alphanumeric or hyphen characters.
    //   - [a-zA-Z0-9]    : Ensures the label ends with an alphanumeric character (prevents trailing hyphens).
    // - \\.              : A literal dot separating domain labels (e.g., in "def.ghi.com").
    // - +                : Ensures there's at least one 'label.' segment before the TLD.

    // Top-Level Domain (TLD):
    // [a-zA-Z]{2,}
    // - [a-zA-Z]{2,}     : The TLD must consist of at least two alphabetic characters (e.g., "com", "uk", "org").

    // $                                  - End of the string.

    const std::string email_pattern =
        "^[a-zA-Z0-9.!#$%&'*+/=?^_`{|}~-]+@"
        "(?:[a-zA-Z0-9](?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]*[a-zA-Z0-9])?\\.)+"
        "[a-zA-Z]{2,}$";

    // Using std::regex_constants::ECMAScript for standard regex syntax.
    std::regex regex_email(email_pattern, std::regex_constants::ECMAScript);

    bool is_valid = std::regex_match(email, regex_email);

    return std::make_tuple(is_valid, email_pattern);
}
